   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Thursday, August 8, 2013 8:23:04 PM UTC-4, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Thursday, August 8, 2013 8:08:58 PM UTC-4, Markian Pahuta wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div>I went through the user manual and mailing lists, and haven&#39;t seen this done... but just wanted to check.</div></div></blockquote><div><br>That is the expected, albeit poor, behavior.<br> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>The following works:</div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"></blockquote><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div></div></blockquote><div><br>It would be much better to invert Sigma once before the loop starts and then use multi_normal_prec, like<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mdivide_left_tri_low</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Eye</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal_prec</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>where Eye is defined as an identity matrix in the transformed data block.<br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>That should have been <br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><code><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#606\"><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\"> mdivide_left_tri_low</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span></span><span style=\"color:#606\"><span style=\"color:#000\">cholesky_decompose</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></span><span style=\"color:#606\"><span style=\"color:#606\">Eye</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></span></code></div></code></div><br>Of course, if Sigma is known, then invert it once in transformed data instead of in the model block.<br><br>Ben<br><br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "nZIM9pkRUgYJ",
   "from": "Marcus Brubaker",
   "author": "2a7f346506a3e9ff",
   "date": "2013-08-09T01:56:06Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Re: Vectorization of Mulivariate Normal",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">We should expose the mdivide_left_spd function which is what is really needed here.  Not sure why it&#39;s not exposed right now, I probably forgot to finish the user visible parts.<div><br></div><div>That said, the code Ben provided might actually be a bit slower, depending on the size of Sigma and how many observations there are.  cholesky_decompose and inverse do not have explicit derivatives, so they fall back to the (much slower) Eigen code paths.</div>\r\n<div><br></div><div>I would guess that:</div><div><br></div><div>Sigma_chol &lt;- cholesky_decompose(Sigma);</div><div>for (a in 1:100) x[a] ~ multi_normal_chol(mu,Sigma_chol);</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Marcus</div>\r\n</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Thu, Aug 8, 2013 at 8:24 PM, Ben Goodrich <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">goodri...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>On Thursday, August 8, 2013 8:23:04 PM UTC-4, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">On Thursday, August 8, 2013 8:08:58 PM UTC-4, Markian Pahuta wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div>I went through the user manual and mailing lists, and haven&#39;t seen this done... but just wanted to check.</div>\r\n</div></blockquote><div><br>That is the expected, albeit poor, behavior.<br> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>The following works:</div>\r\n<blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"></blockquote><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span>data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>\r\n<br>vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span><br>\r\n<br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br><br>model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>a </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span><br>\r\n<br>x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> multi_normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n<br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br></span></div></code></div></div></blockquote><div><br>It would be much better to invert Sigma once before the loop starts and then use multi_normal_prec, like<br>\r\n<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>\r\n  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span>K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> mdivide_left_tri_low</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Eye</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>a </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> multi_normal_prec</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br></span></div></code></div><br>where Eye is defined as an identity matrix in the transformed data block.<br></div></div></blockquote></div><div><br>That should have been <br>\r\n<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><code><span></span><span style=\"color:#606\"><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma_inv</span></span><span><span> </span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span></span><span><span> mdivide_left_tri_low</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span></span><span style=\"color:#606\"><span style=\"color:#000\">cholesky_decompose</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></span><span><span> </span></span><span style=\"color:#606\"><span style=\"color:#606\">Eye</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span></span><span><span><br>\r\n</span></span></code></div></code></div><br>Of course, if Sigma is known, then invert it once in transformed data instead of in the model block.<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br><br>Ben<br><br><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<div><br></div></div></font></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;stan users mailing list&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467957059000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1DVYGFdtRF5OJlaA_MNCQa\">https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out</a>.<br>\r\n <br>\r\n <br>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div><br></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
